#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: description
#, no-wrap
msgid "A free IBM course on quantum information and computation"
msgstr "Un curso gratuito de IBM sobre información cuántica y computación cuántica"

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#, no-wrap
msgid "Density matrix basics"
msgstr "Fundamentos de las matrices de densidad"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"{/* cspell:ignore operatorname */}\n"
"{/* cspell:ignore semidefiniteness */}\n"
msgstr ""
"{/* cspell:ignore operatorname */}\n"
"{/* cspell:ignore semidefiniteness */}\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We'll begin by describing what density matrices are in mathematical terms, and then we'll take a look at some examples.\n"
"After that, we'll discuss a few basic aspects of how density matrices work and how they relate to quantum state vectors in the simplified formulation of quantum information.\n"
msgstr ""
"Comenzamos describiendo las matrices de densidad en términos matemáticos y luego consideramos algunos ejemplos.\n"
"Después discutimos algunos aspectos fundamentales de cómo funcionan las matrices de densidad y su relación con los vectores de estado cuántico en la formulación simplificada de la información cuántica.\n"

#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Definition {#definition}"
msgstr "Definición {#definition}"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Suppose that we have a quantum system named $\\mathsf{X}$, and let $\\Sigma$ be the (finite and nonempty) classical state set of this system.\n"
"Here we're mirroring the naming conventions used in the \"Basics of quantum information\" course, which we'll continue to do when the opportunity arises.\n"
msgstr ""
"Supongamos que tenemos un sistema cuántico llamado $\\mathsf{X}$, y $\\Sigma$ es el conjunto (finito y no vacío) de estados clásicos de este sistema.\n"
"Aquí utilizamos las convenciones de nomenclatura del curso \"Fundamentos de la información cuántica\", que mantendremos siempre que se presente la oportunidad.\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In the general formulation of quantum information, a quantum state of the system $\\mathsf{X}$ is described by a *density matrix* $\\rho$ whose entries are complex numbers and whose indices (for both its rows and columns) have been placed in correspondence with the classical state set $\\Sigma$.\n"
"The lowercase Greek letter $\\rho$ is a conventional first choice for the name of a density matrix, although $\\sigma$ and $\\xi$ are also common choices.\n"
msgstr ""
"En la formulación general de la información cuántica, un estado cuántico del sistema $\\mathsf{X}$ se describe mediante una *matriz de densidad* $\\rho$ cuyos elementos son números complejos y cuyos índices (tanto de filas como de columnas) se han puesto en correspondencia con el conjunto de estados clásicos $\\Sigma$.\n"
"La letra griega minúscula $\\rho$ es la primera opción habitual para nombrar una matriz de densidad, aunque $\\sigma$ y $\\xi$ también son comunes.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Here are a few examples of density matrices that describe states of qubits:\n"
msgstr "Aquí hay algunos ejemplos de matrices de densidad que describen estados de qubits:\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"1 & 0\\\\\n"
"0 & 0\n"
"\\end{pmatrix},\n"
"\\quad\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2}\\\\[2mm]\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2}\n"
"\\end{pmatrix},\n"
"\\quad\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"\\frac{3}{4} & \\frac{i}{8}\\\\[2mm]\n"
"-\\frac{i}{8} & \\frac{1}{4}\n"
"\\end{pmatrix},\n"
"\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} & 0\\\\[2mm]\n"
"0 & \\frac{1}{2}\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"1 & 0\\\\\n"
"0 & 0\n"
"\\end{pmatrix},\n"
"\\quad\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2}\\\\[2mm]\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2}\n"
"\\end{pmatrix},\n"
"\\quad\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"\\frac{3}{4} & \\frac{i}{8}\\\\[2mm]\n"
"-\\frac{i}{8} & \\frac{1}{4}\n"
"\\end{pmatrix},\n"
"\\quad\\text{y}\\quad\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} & 0\\\\[2mm]\n"
"0 & \\frac{1}{2}\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}.\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "To say that $\\rho$ is a density matrix means that these two conditions, which will be explained momentarily, are both satisfied:\n"
msgstr "Decir que $\\rho$ es una matriz de densidad significa que estas dos condiciones, que se explican a continuación, se satisfacen ambas:\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Bullet: '1. '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Unit trace: $\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho) = 1$.\n"
msgstr "Traza unitaria: $\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho) = 1.$\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Bullet: '2. '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Positive semidefiniteness: $\\rho \\geq 0$.\n"
msgstr "Semidefinitud positiva: $\\rho \\geq 0.$\n"

#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "The trace of a matrix {#the-trace-of-a-matrix}"
msgstr "La traza de una matriz {#the-trace-of-a-matrix}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The first condition on density matrices refers to the *trace* of a matrix.\n"
"This is a function that is defined, for all square matrices, as the sum of the diagonal entries:\n"
msgstr ""
"La primera condición sobre las matrices de densidad se refiere a la *traza* de una matriz.\n"
"Esta es una función definida para todas las matrices cuadradas como la suma de los elementos diagonales:\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\operatorname{Tr}\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"\\alpha_{0,0} & \\alpha_{0,1} & \\cdots & \\alpha_{0,n-1}\\\\[1.5mm]\n"
"\\alpha_{1,0} & \\alpha_{1,1} & \\cdots & \\alpha_{1,n-1}\\\\[1.5mm]\n"
"\\vdots & \\vdots & \\ddots & \\vdots\\\\[1.5mm]\n"
"\\alpha_{n-1,0} & \\alpha_{n-1,1} & \\cdots & \\alpha_{n-1,n-1}\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"= \\alpha_{0,0} + \\alpha_{1,1} + \\cdots + \\alpha_{n-1,n-1}.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\operatorname{Tr}\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"\\alpha_{0,0} & \\alpha_{0,1} & \\cdots & \\alpha_{0,n-1}\\\\[1.5mm]\n"
"\\alpha_{1,0} & \\alpha_{1,1} & \\cdots & \\alpha_{1,n-1}\\\\[1.5mm]\n"
"\\vdots & \\vdots & \\ddots & \\vdots\\\\[1.5mm]\n"
"\\alpha_{n-1,0} & \\alpha_{n-1,1} & \\cdots & \\alpha_{n-1,n-1}\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"= \\alpha_{0,0} + \\alpha_{1,1} + \\cdots + \\alpha_{n-1,n-1}.\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The trace is a *linear* function: for any two square matrices $A$ and $B$ of the same size, and any two complex numbers $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$, the following equation is always true.\n"
msgstr "La traza es una función *lineal*: para cualesquiera dos matrices cuadradas $A$ y $B$ del mismo tamaño y dos números complejos $\\alpha$ y $\\beta$, siempre se cumple la siguiente ecuación.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\operatorname{Tr}(\\alpha A + \\beta B) = \\alpha \\operatorname{Tr}(A) + \\beta\\operatorname{Tr}(B)\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\operatorname{Tr}(\\alpha A + \\beta B) = \\alpha \\operatorname{Tr}(A) + \\beta\\operatorname{Tr}(B)\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The trace is an extremely important function and there's a lot more that can be said about it, but we'll wait until the need arises to say more.\n"
msgstr "La traza es una función extremadamente importante, y hay mucho más que decir sobre ella -- sin embargo, pospondremos eso para el momento en que sea necesario.\n"

#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Positive semidefinite matrices {#positive-semidefinite-matrices}"
msgstr "Matrices semidefinidas positivas {#positive-semidefinite-matrices}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The second condition refers to the property of a matrix being *positive semidefinite*, which is a fundamental concept in quantum information theory and in many other subjects.\n"
"A matrix $P$ is *positive semidefinite* if there exists a matrix $M$ such that\n"
msgstr ""
"La segunda condición se refiere a la propiedad de una matriz de ser *semidefinida positiva*, que es un concepto fundamental en la teoría de la información cuántica y muchas otras áreas.\n"
"Una matriz $P$ es *semidefinida positiva* si existe una matriz $M$ tal que\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"P = M^{\\dagger} M.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"P = M^{\\dagger} M.\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Here we can either demand that $M$ is a square matrix of the same size as $P$ or allow it to be non-square — we obtain the same class of matrices either way.\n"
msgstr "Aquí podemos requerir que $M$ sea una matriz cuadrada del mismo tamaño que $P$, o permitir que sea no cuadrada -- en ambos casos obtenemos la misma clase de matrices.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "There are several alternative (but equivalent) ways to define this condition, including these:\n"
msgstr "Existen varias formas alternativas (pero equivalentes) de definir esta condición, entre ellas:\n"

#. type: Bullet: ' - '
#, no-wrap
msgid "A matrix $P$ is positive semidefinite if and only if $P$ is Hermitian (that is, equal to its own conjugate transpose) and all of its eigenvalues are nonnegative real numbers. Checking that a matrix is Hermitian and all of its eigenvalues are nonnegative is a simple computational way to verify that it's positive semidefinite.\n"
msgstr "Una matriz $P$ es semidefinida positiva si y solo si $P$ es hermitiana (es decir, igual a su propia traspuesta conjugada) y todos sus valores propios son números reales no negativos. Verificar si una matriz es hermitiana y si todos sus valores propios son no negativos es un método computacional sencillo para verificar la semidefinitud positiva.\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Bullet: ' - '
#, no-wrap
msgid "A matrix $P$ is positive semidefinite if and only if $\\langle \\psi \\vert P \\vert \\psi \\rangle \\geq 0$ for every complex vector $\\vert\\psi\\rangle$ having the same indices as the rows and columns of $P$.\n"
msgstr "Una matriz $P$ es semidefinida positiva si y solo si $\\langle \\psi \\vert P \\vert \\psi \\rangle \\geq 0$ para todo vector complejo $\\vert\\psi\\rangle$ que tenga los mismos índices que las filas y columnas de $P$.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"An intuitive way to think about positive semidefinite matrices is that they're like matrix analogues of nonnegative real numbers.\n"
"That is, positive semidefinite matrices are to complex square matrices as nonnegative real numbers are to complex numbers.\n"
"For example, a complex number $\\alpha$ is a nonnegative real number if and only if\n"
msgstr ""
"Una forma intuitiva de pensar en las matrices semidefinidas positivas es considerarlas como análogos matriciales de los números reales no negativos.\n"
"Es decir, las matrices semidefinidas positivas son a las matrices cuadradas complejas lo que los números reales no negativos son a los números complejos.\n"
"Por ejemplo, un número complejo $\\alpha$ es un número real no negativo si y solo si\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\alpha = \\overline{\\beta} \\beta\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\alpha = \\overline{\\beta} \\beta\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"for some complex number $\\beta$, which matches the definition of positive semidefiniteness when we replace matrices with scalars.\n"
"While matrices are more complicated objects than scalars in general, this is nevertheless a helpful way to think about positive semidefinite matrices.\n"
msgstr ""
"para algún número complejo $\\beta$, lo cual corresponde a la definición de la propiedad semidefinida positiva cuando reemplazamos matrices por escalares.\n"
"Aunque las matrices son en general objetos más complicados que los escalares, esta sigue siendo una forma útil de pensar en las matrices semidefinidas positivas.\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This also explains the common notation $P\\geq 0$, which indicates that $P$ is positive semidefinite.\n"
"Notice in particular that $P\\geq 0$ does *not* mean that each entry of $P$ is nonnegative in this context;\n"
"there are positive semidefinite matrices having negative entries, as well as matrices whose entries are all positive that are not positive semidefinite.\n"
msgstr ""
"Esto también explica la notación habitual $P\\geq 0$, que indica que $P$ es semidefinida positiva.\n"
"Nótese en particular que $P\\geq 0$ en este contexto *no* significa que cada elemento de $P$ sea no negativo;\n"
"existen matrices semidefinidas positivas con elementos negativos, así como matrices cuyos elementos son todos positivos pero que no son semidefinidas positivas.\n"

#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Interpretation of density matrices {#interpretation-of-density-matrices}"
msgstr "Interpretación de las matrices de densidad {#interpretation-of-density-matrices}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"At this point, the definition of density matrices may seem rather arbitrary and abstract, as we have not yet associated any meaning with these matrices or their entries.\n"
"The way density matrices work and can be interpreted will be clarified as the lesson continues, but for now it may be helpful to think about the entries of density matrices in the following (somewhat informal) way.\n"
msgstr ""
"En este punto, la definición de matrices de densidad puede parecer bastante arbitraria y abstracta, ya que aún no hemos asignado significado a estas matrices o a sus elementos.\n"
"Cómo funcionan e interpretan las matrices de densidad se aclarará a lo largo de la lección, pero por ahora puede ser útil pensar en los elementos de las matrices de densidad de la siguiente manera (algo informal).\n"

#. type: Bullet: ' - '
#, no-wrap
msgid "The *diagonal* entries of a density matrix give us the probabilities for each classical state to appear if we perform a standard basis measurement — so we can think about these entries as describing the \"weight\" or \"likelihood\" associated with each classical state.\n"
msgstr "Los elementos *diagonales* de una matriz de densidad nos dan las probabilidades de que cada estado clásico aparezca en una medición de la base estándar -- por lo que podemos considerar estos elementos como una descripción del \"peso\" o la \"probabilidad\" asociada a cada estado clásico.\n"

#. type: Bullet: ' - '
#, no-wrap
msgid "The *off-diagonal* entries of a density matrix describe the degree to which the two classical states corresponding to that entry (meaning the one corresponding to the row and the one corresponding to the column) are in quantum superposition, as well as the relative phase between them.\n"
msgstr "Los elementos *fuera de la diagonal* de una matriz de densidad describen el grado en que los dos estados clásicos correspondientes a ese elemento (el de la fila y el de la columna) se encuentran en superposición cuántica, así como la fase relativa entre ellos.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"It is certainly not obvious *a priori* that quantum states should be represented by density matrices.\n"
"Indeed, there is a sense in which the choice to represent quantum states by density matrices leads naturally to the entire mathematical description of quantum information.\n"
"Everything else about quantum information actually follows pretty logically from this one choice!\n"
msgstr ""
"Ciertamente no es obvio a priori que los estados cuánticos deban representarse mediante matrices de densidad.\n"
"De hecho, existe un sentido en el que la elección de representar los estados cuánticos mediante matrices de densidad conduce naturalmente a toda la descripción matemática de la información cuántica.\n"
"Todo lo demás sobre la información cuántica se deduce de manera bastante lógica a partir de esta única elección.\n"

#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Connection to quantum state vectors {#connection-to-quantum-state-vectors}"
msgstr "Conexión con los vectores de estado cuántico {#connection-to-quantum-state-vectors}"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Recall that a quantum state vector $\\vert\\psi\\rangle$ describing a quantum state of $\\mathsf{X}$ is a column vector having Euclidean norm equal to $1$ whose entries have been placed in correspondence with the classical state set $\\Sigma$.\n"
"The density matrix representation $\\rho$ of the same state is defined as follows.\n"
msgstr ""
"Recordemos que un vector de estado cuántico $\\vert\\psi\\rangle$, que describe un estado cuántico de $\\mathsf{X}$, es un vector columna con norma euclidiana igual a $1$ cuyos elementos se han puesto en correspondencia con el conjunto de estados clásicos $\\Sigma$.\n"
"La representación como matriz de densidad $\\rho$ del mismo estado se define de la siguiente manera.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\rho = \\vert\\psi\\rangle\\langle\\psi\\vert\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\rho = \\vert\\psi\\rangle\\langle\\psi\\vert\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"To be clear, we're multiplying a column vector to a row vector, so the result is a square matrix whose rows and columns correspond to $\\Sigma$.\n"
"Matrices of this form, in addition to being density matrices, are always projections and have rank equal to $1$.\n"
msgstr ""
"Para ser claros: multiplicamos un vector columna por un vector fila, de modo que el resultado es una matriz cuadrada cuyas filas y columnas corresponden a $\\Sigma$.\n"
"Las matrices de esta forma, además de ser matrices de densidad, siempre son proyecciones y tienen rango igual a $1$.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "For example, let's define two qubit state vectors.\n"
msgstr "Como ejemplo, definamos dos vectores de estado de qubit.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"\\vert {+i} \\rangle & = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\vert 0 \\rangle + \\frac{i}{\\sqrt{2}} \\vert 1 \\rangle\n"
"= \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\[2mm] \\frac{i}{\\sqrt{2}} \\end{pmatrix} \\\\[5mm]\n"
"\\vert {-i} \\rangle & = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\vert 0 \\rangle - \\frac{i}{\\sqrt{2}} \\vert 1 \\rangle\n"
"= \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\[2mm] -\\frac{i}{\\sqrt{2}} \\end{pmatrix}\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"\\vert {+i} \\rangle & = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\vert 0 \\rangle + \\frac{i}{\\sqrt{2}} \\vert 1 \\rangle\n"
"= \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\[2mm] \\frac{i}{\\sqrt{2}} \\end{pmatrix} \\\\[5mm]\n"
"\\vert {-i} \\rangle & = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\vert 0 \\rangle - \\frac{i}{\\sqrt{2}} \\vert 1 \\rangle\n"
"= \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\[2mm] -\\frac{i}{\\sqrt{2}} \\end{pmatrix}\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The density matrices corresponding to these two vectors are as follows.\n"
msgstr "Las matrices de densidad correspondientes a estos dos vectores son las siguientes.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"\\vert {+i} \\rangle\\langle{+i}\\vert\n"
"& = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\[2mm] \\frac{i}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & - \\frac{i}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"= \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{i}{2}\\\\[2mm]\n"
"\\frac{i}{2} & \\frac{1}{2}\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\\\\[5mm]\n"
"\\vert {-i} \\rangle\\langle{-i}\\vert\n"
"& = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\[2mm] -\\frac{i}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{i}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"= \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{i}{2}\\\\[2mm]\n"
"-\\frac{i}{2} & \\frac{1}{2}\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"\\vert {+i} \\rangle\\langle{+i}\\vert\n"
"& = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\[2mm] \\frac{i}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & - \\frac{i}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"= \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{i}{2}\\\\[2mm]\n"
"\\frac{i}{2} & \\frac{1}{2}\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\\\\[5mm]\n"
"\\vert {-i} \\rangle\\langle{-i}\\vert\n"
"& = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\[2mm] -\\frac{i}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{i}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"= \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{i}{2}\\\\[2mm]\n"
"-\\frac{i}{2} & \\frac{1}{2}\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Here's a table listing these states along with a few other basic examples: $\\vert 0\\rangle$, $\\vert 1\\rangle$, $\\vert {+}\\rangle$, and\n"
"$\\vert {-}\\rangle$.\n"
"We'll see these six states again later in the lesson.\n"
msgstr ""
"A continuación se muestra una tabla con estos estados y algunos otros ejemplos básicos: $\\vert 0\\rangle,$ $\\vert 1\\rangle,$ $\\vert {+}\\rangle,$ y\n"
"$\\vert {-}\\rangle.$\n"
"Volveremos a encontrar estos seis estados más adelante en la lección.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"| State vector | Density matrix |\n"
"| :---: | :---: |\n"
"| $$\\vert 0\\rangle = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\[1mm] 0 \\end{pmatrix}$$ | $$\\vert 0\\rangle\\langle 0\\vert = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0\\\\[1mm] 0 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}$$ |\n"
"| $$\\vert 1\\rangle = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\[1mm] 1 \\end{pmatrix}$$ | $$\\vert 1\\rangle\\langle 1\\vert = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0\\\\[1mm] 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}$$ |\n"
"| $$\\vert {+}\\rangle = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\[2mm] \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\end{pmatrix}$$ | $$\\vert {+}\\rangle\\langle {+}\\vert = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2}\\\\[2mm] \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} \\end{pmatrix}$$ |\n"
"| $$\\vert {-} \\rangle = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\[2mm] -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{pmatrix}$$ | $$\\vert {-}\\rangle\\langle {-}\\vert = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2}\\\\[2mm] -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} \\end{pmatrix}$$ |\n"
"| $$\\vert {+i} \\rangle = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\[2mm] \\frac{i}{\\sqrt{2}} \\end{pmatrix}$$ | $$\\vert {+i} \\rangle\\langle {+i} \\vert = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{i}{2}\\\\[2mm] \\frac{i}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} \\end{pmatrix}$$ |\n"
"| $$\\vert {-i} \\rangle = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\[2mm] -\\frac{i}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{pmatrix}$$ | $$\\vert {-i} \\rangle\\langle {-i} \\vert = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{i}{2}\\\\[2mm] -\\frac{i}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} \\end{pmatrix}$$ |\n"
msgstr ""
"| Vector de estado | Matriz de densidad |\n"
"| :---: | :---: |\n"
"| $$\\vert 0\\rangle = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\[1mm] 0 \\end{pmatrix}$$ | $$\\vert 0\\rangle\\langle 0\\vert = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0\\\\[1mm] 0 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}$$ |\n"
"| $$\\vert 1\\rangle = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\[1mm] 1 \\end{pmatrix}$$ | $$\\vert 1\\rangle\\langle 1\\vert = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0\\\\[1mm] 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}$$ |\n"
"| $$\\vert {+}\\rangle = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\[2mm] \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\end{pmatrix}$$ | $$\\vert {+}\\rangle\\langle {+}\\vert = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2}\\\\[2mm] \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} \\end{pmatrix}$$ |\n"
"| $$\\vert {-} \\rangle = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\[2mm] -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{pmatrix}$$ | $$\\vert {-}\\rangle\\langle {-}\\vert = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2}\\\\[2mm] -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} \\end{pmatrix}$$ |\n"
"| $$\\vert {+i} \\rangle = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\[2mm] \\frac{i}{\\sqrt{2}} \\end{pmatrix}$$ | $$\\vert {+i} \\rangle\\langle {+i} \\vert = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{i}{2}\\\\[2mm] \\frac{i}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} \\end{pmatrix}$$ |\n"
"| $$\\vert {-i} \\rangle = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\[2mm] -\\frac{i}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{pmatrix}$$ | $$\\vert {-i} \\rangle\\langle {-i} \\vert = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{i}{2}\\\\[2mm] -\\frac{i}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} \\end{pmatrix}$$ |\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "For one more example, here's a state from the *Single systems* lesson of the \"Basics of quantum information\" course, including both its state vector and density matrix representations.\n"
msgstr "Como otro ejemplo, consideremos un estado de la lección *Sistemas individuales* del curso \"Fundamentos de la información cuántica\", incluyendo ambas representaciones como vector de estado y como matriz de densidad.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\vert v\\rangle = \\frac{1 + 2 i}{3}\\,\\vert 0\\rangle - \\frac{2}{3}\\,\\vert 1\\rangle\n"
"\\qquad\n"
"\\vert v\\rangle\\langle v\\vert =\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"  \\frac{5}{9} & \\frac{-2 - 4 i}{9}\\\\[2mm]\n"
"  \\frac{-2 + 4 i}{9} & \\frac{4}{9}\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\vert v\\rangle = \\frac{1 + 2 i}{3}\\,\\vert 0\\rangle - \\frac{2}{3}\\,\\vert 1\\rangle\n"
"\\qquad\n"
"\\vert v\\rangle\\langle v\\vert =\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"  \\frac{5}{9} & \\frac{-2 - 4 i}{9}\\\\[2mm]\n"
"  \\frac{-2 + 4 i}{9} & \\frac{4}{9}\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Density matrices that take the form $\\rho = \\vert \\psi \\rangle \\langle \\psi \\vert$ for a quantum state vector $\\vert \\psi \\rangle$ are known as *pure states*.\n"
"Not every density matrix can be written in this form; some states are not pure.\n"
msgstr ""
"Las matrices de densidad que toman la forma $\\rho = \\vert \\psi \\rangle \\langle \\psi \\vert$ para un vector de estado cuántico $\\vert \\psi \\rangle$ se denominan *estados puros*.\n"
"No toda matriz de densidad puede escribirse en esta forma; algunos estados no son puros.\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"As density matrices, pure states always have one eigenvalue equal to $1$ and all other eigenvalues equal to $0$.\n"
"This is consistent with the interpretation that the eigenvalues of a density matrix describe the randomness or uncertainty inherent to that state.\n"
"In essence, there's no uncertainty for a pure state $\\rho = \\vert \\psi \\rangle \\langle \\psi \\vert$ — the state is definitely $\\vert \\psi \\rangle$.\n"
msgstr ""
"Como matrices de densidad, los estados puros siempre tienen un valor propio igual a $1$ y todos los demás valores propios iguales a $0$.\n"
"Esto es consistente con la interpretación de que los valores propios de una matriz de densidad describen la aleatoriedad o incertidumbre inherente a los estados que representan.\n"
"Esencialmente, no hay incertidumbre para un estado puro $\\rho = \\vert \\psi \\rangle \\langle \\psi \\vert$ -- el estado es definitivamente $\\vert \\psi \\rangle$.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In general, for a quantum state vector\n"
msgstr "En general, para un vector de estado cuántico\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\vert\\psi\\rangle =\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"\\alpha_0\\\\\n"
"\\alpha_1\\\\\n"
"\\vdots\\\\\n"
"\\alpha_{n-1}\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\vert\\psi\\rangle =\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"\\alpha_0\\\\\n"
"\\alpha_1\\\\\n"
"\\vdots\\\\\n"
"\\alpha_{n-1}\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "for a system with $n$ classical states, the density matrix representation of the same state is as follows.\n"
msgstr "para un sistema con $n$ estados clásicos, la representación como matriz de densidad del mismo estado es la siguiente.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"\\vert\\psi\\rangle\\langle\\psi\\vert\n"
"& =\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"\\alpha_0 \\overline{\\alpha_0} & \\alpha_0 \\overline{\\alpha_1} & \\cdots & \\alpha_0 \\overline{\\alpha_{n-1}}\\\\[1mm]\n"
"\\alpha_1 \\overline{\\alpha_0} & \\alpha_1 \\overline{\\alpha_1} & \\cdots & \\alpha_1 \\overline{\\alpha_{n-1}}\\\\[1mm]\n"
"\\vdots & \\vdots & \\ddots & \\vdots\\\\[1mm]\n"
"\\alpha_{n-1} \\overline{\\alpha_0} & \\alpha_{n-1} \\overline{\\alpha_1} & \\cdots & \\alpha_{n-1} \\overline{\\alpha_{n-1}}\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\\\\[10mm]\n"
"& = \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"\\vert\\alpha_0\\vert^2 & \\alpha_0 \\overline{\\alpha_1} & \\cdots & \\alpha_0 \\overline{\\alpha_{n-1}}\\\\[1mm]\n"
"\\alpha_1 \\overline{\\alpha_0} & \\vert\\alpha_1\\vert^2 & \\cdots & \\alpha_1 \\overline{\\alpha_{n-1}}\\\\[1mm]\n"
"\\vdots & \\vdots & \\ddots & \\vdots\\\\[1mm]\n"
"\\alpha_{n-1} \\overline{\\alpha_0} & \\alpha_{n-1} \\overline{\\alpha_1} & \\cdots & \\vert\\alpha_{n-1}\\vert^2\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"\\vert\\psi\\rangle\\langle\\psi\\vert\n"
"& =\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"\\alpha_0 \\overline{\\alpha_0} & \\alpha_0 \\overline{\\alpha_1} & \\cdots & \\alpha_0 \\overline{\\alpha_{n-1}}\\\\[1mm]\n"
"\\alpha_1 \\overline{\\alpha_0} & \\alpha_1 \\overline{\\alpha_1} & \\cdots & \\alpha_1 \\overline{\\alpha_{n-1}}\\\\[1mm]\n"
"\\vdots & \\vdots & \\ddots & \\vdots\\\\[1mm]\n"
"\\alpha_{n-1} \\overline{\\alpha_0} & \\alpha_{n-1} \\overline{\\alpha_1} & \\cdots & \\alpha_{n-1} \\overline{\\alpha_{n-1}}\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\\\\[10mm]\n"
"& = \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"\\vert\\alpha_0\\vert^2 & \\alpha_0 \\overline{\\alpha_1} & \\cdots & \\alpha_0 \\overline{\\alpha_{n-1}}\\\\[1mm]\n"
"\\alpha_1 \\overline{\\alpha_0} & \\vert\\alpha_1\\vert^2 & \\cdots & \\alpha_1 \\overline{\\alpha_{n-1}}\\\\[1mm]\n"
"\\vdots & \\vdots & \\ddots & \\vdots\\\\[1mm]\n"
"\\alpha_{n-1} \\overline{\\alpha_0} & \\alpha_{n-1} \\overline{\\alpha_1} & \\cdots & \\vert\\alpha_{n-1}\\vert^2\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "So, for the special case of pure states, we can verify that the diagonal entries of a density matrix describe the probabilities that a standard basis measurement would output each possible classical state.\n"
msgstr "En el caso especial de los estados puros, se puede verificar que los elementos diagonales de una matriz de densidad describen las probabilidades de que una medición en la base estándar produzca cada estado clásico posible.\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A final remark about pure states is that density matrices eliminate the degeneracy concerning *global phases* found for quantum state vectors.\n"
"Suppose we have two quantum state vectors that differ by a global phase:\n"
"$\\vert \\psi \\rangle$ and $\\vert \\phi \\rangle = e^{i \\theta} \\vert \\psi \\rangle$, for some real number $\\theta$.\n"
"Because they differ by a global phase, these vectors represent exactly the same quantum state, despite the fact that the vectors may be different.\n"
"The density matrices that we obtain from these two state vectors, on the other hand, are identical.\n"
msgstr ""
"Una observación final sobre los estados puros: las matrices de densidad eliminan la ambigüedad respecto a las *fases globales* que surge con los vectores de estado cuántico.\n"
"Supongamos que tenemos dos vectores de estado cuántico que difieren por una fase global:\n"
"$\\vert \\psi \\rangle$ y $\\vert \\phi \\rangle = e^{i \\theta} \\vert \\psi \\rangle,$ para un número real $\\theta.$\n"
"Dado que difieren por una fase global, estos vectores representan exactamente el mismo estado cuántico, aunque los vectores pueden ser diferentes.\n"
"Las matrices de densidad que obtenemos de estos dos vectores de estado son, sin embargo, idénticas.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\vert \\phi \\rangle \\langle \\phi \\vert =\n"
"\\bigl( e^{i\\theta} \\vert \\psi \\rangle \\bigr) \\bigl( e^{i\\theta} \\vert \\psi \\rangle \\bigr)^{\\dagger}\n"
"= e^{i(\\theta - \\theta)} \\vert \\psi \\rangle \\langle \\psi \\vert\n"
"= \\vert \\psi \\rangle \\langle \\psi \\vert\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\vert \\phi \\rangle \\langle \\phi \\vert =\n"
"\\bigl( e^{i\\theta} \\vert \\psi \\rangle \\bigr) \\bigl( e^{i\\theta} \\vert \\psi \\rangle \\bigr)^{\\dagger}\n"
"= e^{i(\\theta - \\theta)} \\vert \\psi \\rangle \\langle \\psi \\vert\n"
"= \\vert \\psi \\rangle \\langle \\psi \\vert\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In general, density matrices provide a unique representation of quantum states:\n"
"two quantum states are identical, generating exactly the same outcome statistics for every possible measurement that can be performed on them, if and only if their density matrix representations are equal.\n"
"Using mathematical parlance, we can express this by saying that density matrices offer a *faithful* representation of quantum states.\n"
msgstr ""
"En general, las matrices de densidad proporcionan una representación unívoca de los estados cuánticos:\n"
"dos estados cuánticos son identicos y producen exactamente las mismas estadísticas de resultados para cualquier medición posible que se pueda realizar sobre ellos, si y solo si sus representaciones como matrices de densidad son iguales.\n"
"En lenguaje matemático, esto significa que las matrices de densidad proporcionan una representación *fiel* de los estados cuánticos.\n"
